<section class="blk">
<h2 id="h-1-where-the-surname-comes-from">1. Where the surname comes from</h2>
  <p><strong>That <em>Lerena</em> is a Basque-formed house-name of the upper Ebro, not a reduction of Llerena in Badajoz.</strong></p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>For:</strong> Spain has exactly one real cluster of the name and it is <strong>La Rioja</strong>,
      at <strong>39.6&times;</strong> the national density; Andalusia and Madrid sit at 1.4&times;, which is
      merely what population size predicts. La Rioja lies on the upper Ebro, immediately south of the Basque
      provinces, where Basque house-names in <strong>-ena</strong> ("the house of") spill into Castilian
      country. <strong>Extremadura does not appear in the Spanish distribution at all.</strong></li>
    <li><strong>Against:</strong> <em>Llerena</em> has 36,828 bearers and <em>Ll-</em> does reduce to
      <em>L-</em> readily in Spanish-American registers.</li>
    <li><strong>To settle it:</strong> the La Rioja parish registers; and whether <em>Lerena</em> and
      <em>Llerena</em> ever alternate for one person in early River Plate records.</li>
  </ul>
  <div class="panel" style="border-left:3px solid var(--gold, var(--accent));margin:22px 0">
    <p class="lab" style="margin:0 0 8px">15 September 2026 &mdash; the marriage act, found and read</p>
    <div class="prose">
      <p>Everything below this panel was written without the one document the whole question turned on.
        <strong>It has now been read.</strong> Canelones, Parroquia de Nuestra Se&ntilde;ora de Guadalupe,
        <em>Matrimonios Libro I</em> &mdash; <strong>DGS 8166578, image 2687</strong>, left-hand page,
        second act. The margin reads <em>&ldquo;Sancho Llerena, y Mar&iacute;a Fernandes. Casados y
        Velados&rdquo;</em>.</p>
      <blockquote class="rec" style="margin:14px 0"><em>En ocho d&iacute;as del Mes de Enero de
        <strong>1791</strong> a&ntilde;os, haviendo precedido justificaci&oacute;n de libertad de
        <strong>Sancho Llerena, nat[ura]l de C&oacute;rdova la Llana, hijo leg&iacute;timo de F&eacute;lix
        Llerena, y de Rosal&iacute;a Aguilar, nat[ura]les de dicha C&oacute;rdova</strong>&hellip; el
        matrimonio que el dicho Sancho intentava con <strong>Mar&iacute;a Fern&aacute;ndez, nat[ura]l de
        las Asturias, hija leg&iacute;tima de Rodrigo Fern&aacute;ndez, y de Ro[sa] Fern&aacute;ndez,
        naturales de dichas Asturias, y Vecinos de S[an]ta Luc&iacute;a</strong>&hellip;</em></blockquote>
      <p><strong>Four things settle at once.</strong></p>
      <p><strong>The date.</strong> <em>&ldquo;de 1791 a&ntilde;os&rdquo;</em> is written in
        <strong>arabic numerals</strong>, so the <em>noventa</em>-against-<em>ochenta</em> ambiguity that
        has cost this archive days simply does not arise. <strong>8 January 1791</strong> &mdash; exactly
        what Apolant&rsquo;s <em>ficha 355</em> said. Apolant is now corroborated by the act itself.</p>
      <p><strong>C&oacute;rdoba, not Granada.</strong> This archive had C&oacute;rdoba only from unsourced
        Geneanet trees, had a competing Granada reading, and had recorded honestly that C&oacute;rdoba
        <em>&ldquo;rests on one unsourced note&rdquo;</em>. The act says <em>C&oacute;rdova</em>, twice.
        <strong>The trees were right for the wrong reason</strong> &mdash; they agreed with a document none
        of them had seen.</p>
      <p><strong>His parents, for the second time and independently.</strong> The 1811 baptism of his son
        Luis gave <em>&ldquo;Abuelos paternos Feliz Gerena y Rosal&iacute;a Aguilar&rdquo;</em>. Two parish
        acts, twenty years apart, in two different parishes, agree on both names &mdash; and this one adds
        what 1811 could not: <strong>the parents were themselves of C&oacute;rdoba</strong>. That is the
        first evidence placing the generation above Sancho anywhere at all.</p>
      <p><strong>And it corrects the trees on the bride.</strong> The Geneanet note recorded here called her
        father <em>&ldquo;Ram&oacute;n Fern&aacute;ndez Tresguerras&rdquo;</em>. The act says
        <strong>Rodrigo</strong> &mdash; which is also what the 1811 baptism says. Where a tree and an act
        disagree, the act wins. The act also makes her family <em>&ldquo;Vecinos de Santa Luc&iacute;a&rdquo;</em>,
        which is new, and the 1812 padr&oacute;n later puts Rodrigo Fern&aacute;ndez, 63,
        <em>asturiano</em>, living in Sancho&rsquo;s own household.</p>
      <div class="note"><strong>And the puzzling word has been run down &mdash; it is the city itself.</strong>
        The qualifier after <em>C&oacute;rdova</em> reads <em>&ldquo;la Llana&rdquo;</em>, and no
        municipality of that name exists because it is not a municipality:
        <strong><em>&ldquo;C&oacute;rdoba la llana&rdquo;</em> is a standing epithet for the city of
        C&oacute;rdoba</strong> in Andalusia. So the act names <strong>the city</strong>, not a village
        in its province. <strong>And the qualifier is doing real work.</strong> In the River Plate
        <em>C&oacute;rdoba</em> was ambiguous: the regiment&rsquo;s own chaplaincy book at Montevideo
        describes a man in the very same years as <em>&ldquo;natural de C&oacute;rdova <strong>del
        Tucum&aacute;n</strong>&rdquo;</em>. A priest writing for readers who knew both had to say which
        C&oacute;rdoba he meant &mdash; and <em>la llana</em> says Andalusia. That agrees with the 1811
        baptism, which calls Sancho <em>&ldquo;natural de Andaluc&iacute;a&rdquo;</em>.</div>
      <p class="cap"><strong>Who married them.</strong> <em>Don Juan Francisco Cardoso, Cura Interino</em>
        &mdash; interim because Don Juan Miguel de Laguna, who signed the 1788 <em>informaci&oacute;n</em>,
        is called <em>&ldquo;el finado&rdquo;</em> in an act of February 1789. The witnesses were
        <strong>Antonio Villegas and Jos&eacute; del R&iacute;o</strong> &mdash; and Jos&eacute; del
        R&iacute;o is the same notary who, seven years later, wrote down Sancho&rsquo;s sworn deposition
        and recorded that he could not sign it. <strong>The act gives no age, no occupation and no
        regiment</strong>; &ldquo;born about 1767&rdquo; is still only a tree. Full transcription in
        <code>data/1791-marriage-act.tsv</code>.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="panel" style="border-left:3px solid var(--terra);margin:22px 0">
    <p class="lab" style="margin:0 0 8px">13 September 2026 &mdash; the test was met, and it answered the
      other way</p>
    <div class="prose">
      <p>The line above says what would settle it: <em>whether</em> Lerena <em>and</em> Llerena
        <em>ever alternate for one person in early River Plate records</em>. Following the 1866 Rosario
        act back through the Montevideo family produced a trunk, and at its root:</p>
      <p style="font-size:1.05rem"><strong>Sancho Llerena Aguilar</strong>, born
        <strong>Córdoba, Andalusia, 1767</strong>; a soldier; married at <strong>Canelones, Uruguay,
        8 January 1791</strong>; resident at Montevideo; died 1832. Great-grandfather of both the
        <strong>Lerena Lenguas</strong> and the <strong>Lerena Traibel</strong> &mdash; and so of the
        Luis Lerena Lenguas named in the Rosario act.</p>
      <p>Fourteen Uruguayan parish records are attached to him. They spell him <strong>Lerena</strong> in
        1793, 1840 and 1858; <strong>Llerena</strong> in 1804, 1811, 1827 and 1830; and once, in 1828,
        <strong><em>Sancho de Llerena</em></strong>.</p>
      <p><strong>That last form is the strongest item in the list.</strong> <em>De Llerena</em> is the
        toponymic construction &mdash; <em>of Llerena</em>, the Badajoz town &mdash; and a Basque
        house-name in <em>-ena</em> does not take <em>de</em>.</p>
      <p><strong>It does not overturn the distribution.</strong> La Rioja really is where the spelling
        clusters in Spain today, and that cluster has to be something. The honest synthesis is that
        <strong>both can be true at once</strong>: a Basque-formed <em>Lerena</em> of the upper Ebro,
        and separately a River Plate <em>Lerena</em> that is a reduction of Andalusian <em>Llerena</em>,
        carried over by a soldier from Córdoba and smoothed to one <em>l</em> by Uruguayan clerks within
        a generation. <strong>For this archive only the second matters</strong> &mdash; the question was
        never where the name is dense in Spain, but where <em>this family&rsquo;s</em> name came from.</p>
      <p class="cap">Tier, as written on 8 September: the trunk and the attachments are
        <strong>secondary</strong> &mdash; FamilySearch Tree is user-submitted, and nobody here had
        opened the fourteen acts to confirm they are one man. What is checkable by anyone who looks is the narrower fact: the Uruguayan parish
        registers spell this family <strong>both ways</strong>. And <strong>no Juan Carlos appears
        anywhere in that tree.</strong></p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="panel" style="border-left:3px solid var(--accent);margin:22px 0">
    <p class="lab" style="margin:0 0 8px">13&ndash;14 September 2026 &mdash; Sancho stopped being
      secondary, after first appearing to collapse altogether</p>
    <div class="prose">
      <p><strong>He nearly went.</strong> Two baptism entries of 1846, read on 13 September, name Luis
        Lerena&rsquo;s father not as Sancho but as <strong><em>Francisco</em></strong>. On those two
        acts the Andalusian root of this whole tree looked wrong.</p>
      <p>Three acts now name him, and the earliest is the plainest:</p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>3 September 1836</strong> &mdash; Luis Lerena&rsquo;s own marriage petition at
          Montevideo, on the stamped paper of that year and <strong>over his own signature</strong>:
          <em>&ldquo;hijo leg&iacute;timo de D. <strong>Sancho Lerena</strong> y de D&ordf; Mar&iacute;a
          Fern&aacute;ndez&rdquo;</em>.</li>
        <li><strong>3 March 1842</strong> &mdash; his second marriage, to Justiniana Lenguas:
          <em>&ldquo;hijo leg&iacute;timo de D. <strong>Sancho</strong> y D&ordf; Mar&iacute;a
          Fern&aacute;ndez&rdquo;</em>.</li>
        <li><strong>7 October 1850</strong> &mdash; the baptism of his son Alberto Octavio:
          <em>&ldquo;Paternos: D. <strong>Sancho</strong> y D&ordf; Mar&iacute;a Fern&aacute;ndez&rdquo;</em>.</li>
      </ul>
      <div class="note" style="margin-top:14px"><strong>That argument stood for about an hour, and it
        does not hold.</strong> It said the two dissenting 1846 entries disqualify themselves &mdash; each
        ends <em>&ldquo;as&iacute; consta del Legajo conservado en este Archivo&rdquo;</em>, so they are
        later <strong>transcripts</strong>, and three originals against two copies is no conflict.
        <strong>Then an original turned up saying Francisco.</strong> The Catedral baptism of October
        1833, for a second child of his daughter Justa Pastora, names her parents
        <em>&ldquo;Maternos: <strong>Francisco Lerina</strong> y Mar&iacute;a Fern&aacute;ndez&rdquo;</em>.
        It is not a copy.</div>
      <p style="margin-top:14px"><strong>What is constant is the wife.</strong> <em>Mar&iacute;a
        Fern&aacute;ndez</em> is in every one of these records without exception, across seventy years
        and both spellings of the husband&rsquo;s forename. And the readings split <em>across</em>
        methods rather than along them &mdash; the machine transcript read <em>Sancho</em> in 1831 and
        <em>Francisco</em> in 1833; this archive read <em>Francisco</em> off the 1846 images and
        <em>Sancho</em> off 1836, 1842 and 1850. So it is not simply that the machine is wrong, or that
        we are.</p>
      <p><strong>The likeliest reading is a double forename</strong> &mdash; <em>Sancho Francisco</em>,
        or <em>Francisco Sancho</em> &mdash; with clerks using one or the other, which is ordinary in the
        period and would mean nobody is wrong. Two men of one surname each married to a Mar&iacute;a
        Fern&aacute;ndez is possible and much less likely. <strong>Sancho&rsquo;s own marriage act would
        settle it, and it has never been opened.</strong></p>
      <p class="cap" style="margin-top:14px">One more thing that record would settle: this archive
        calls him <strong>Sancho Llerena <em>Aguilar</em></strong>, and <strong>not one of the eight
        records gives him a matronymic</strong>. A full-text search for <em>&ldquo;Llerena y
        Aguilar&rdquo;</em> returns thirty hits and every one is <strong>Quito, Ecuador</strong> &mdash;
        a notarial family there. <em>Aguilar</em> comes from the FamilySearch Tree and from nowhere this
        archive has read. It is kept, and marked.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="panel" style="border-left:3px solid var(--accent);margin:22px 0">
    <p class="lab" style="margin:0 0 8px">14 September 2026 &mdash; the family enters the national history
      once, and it is not a credit</p>
    <div class="prose">
      <p>A Geneanet note cited Francisco Bauz&aacute;&rsquo;s <em>Historia de la Dominaci&oacute;n
        Espa&ntilde;ola en el Uruguay</em> for the Lerenas of Canelones as <em>&ldquo;persona de confianza
        del Bar&oacute;n de la Laguna en 1817, para vigilar la seguridad y tranquilidad en la
        campa&ntilde;a&rdquo;</em>. Bauz&aacute; is out of copyright and all three volumes are on the
        Internet Archive, so the citation could be <strong>read whole rather than trusted</strong>.
        <strong>It is not what that summary suggests.</strong></p>
      <p>The phrase quoted is <strong>Lecor&rsquo;s own</strong>, from the Bando of <strong>15 February
        1817</strong>, which put resisting Orientals outside the law &mdash; <em>&ldquo;ellos, sus familias
        y sus propiedades&rdquo;</em> &mdash; and sent detachments out to <em>burn their farms and escort
        their families aboard the fleet</em>. <em>&ldquo;Personas de confianza&rdquo;</em> were to identify
        them. The tree stops there. Bauz&aacute; does not:</p>
      <blockquote style="margin:14px 0;padding-left:14px;border-left:3px solid var(--rule, #ccc)">
        <em>&ldquo;Doloroso es manifestar que las mencionadas personas de confianza, fueron los hermanos
        Alonso de Minas, <strong>los Llerena de Canelones</strong>, D. Mart&iacute;n, D. Francisco y D.
        Bernab&eacute; Alb&iacute;n de Colonia, D. Manuel Garc&iacute;a de Montevideo, un Alanengo de San
        Jos&eacute;, un G&aacute;ndara de Maldonado, y un Rocha que serv&iacute;a de principal baqueano al
        intruso; quienes al frente de grupos cuya totalidad sumaba m&aacute;s de 400 hombres, se
        transformaron en <strong>azote de los vecindarios de Toledo, Manga y Piedras</strong>, haciendo
        impunemente con ellos, lo que era castigado como un crimen en los patriotas.&rdquo;</em>
      </blockquote>
      <p>They were not watchmen. They led irregulars for the Portuguese occupation, and Bauz&aacute;
        prefaces the list <em>&ldquo;doloroso es manifestar&rdquo;</em> &mdash; it is painful to state.</p>
      <div class="note" style="margin-top:14px"><strong>What this does not establish.</strong>
        Bauz&aacute; writes <em>los Llerena de Canelones</em> &mdash; a family, in the plural,
        <strong>with no forenames</strong>. <strong>No person in this archive is placed at those events by
        this text.</strong> What can be said is narrow: in 1817 there was a Llerena family at Canelones
        prominent enough for a national historian to name among eight collaborating parties, and this
        archive&rsquo;s Llerena were at Canelones in exactly that generation &mdash; Sancho aged 50, his
        sons Juan Agust&iacute;n 26, Pedro Antonio 24 and Fidel 17 by the 1812 padr&oacute;n. That is a
        strong identification and it remains an identification.</div>
      <p style="margin-top:14px">Two footnotes worth keeping. The same volume has a <strong>D. Jos&eacute;
        Pereira Lerena</strong> serving as an <em>ayudante</em> in Rondeau&rsquo;s <strong>patriot</strong>
        army &mdash; a different compound surname, recorded here so that nobody attaches the wrong man to
        this family in either direction. And <strong>Tomes I and II hold no Lerena at all</strong>, against
        a control of 108 and 503 hits for <em>Montevideo</em> in the same downloaded files.</p>
      <p class="cap">This sits thirty years before the Oribe material this archive already holds, and on
        the other side of the national story.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="panel" style="border-left:3px solid var(--accent);margin:22px 0">
    <p class="lab" style="margin:0 0 8px">14 September 2026 &mdash; &ldquo;Aguilar&rdquo; is answered, and
      it is his mother</p>
    <div class="prose">
      <p>The paragraph above says <em>Aguilar</em> comes from a tree and from nowhere this archive has
        read, and keeps it marked. <strong>That is now answered, and not in the direction this archive
        spent a morning arguing for.</strong></p>
      <p>A Granada marriage of <strong>17 November 1754</strong> looked like the key: Sancho de Lerena y
        Armenta married Ana Mar&iacute;a de Aguilar, and a son of theirs would be styled <em>Lerena y
        Aguilar</em>. That reasoning was a naming rule applied to a name whose owner is recorded
        elsewhere. <strong>The Geneanet tree of jfina &mdash; one of the four this archive cites for the
        compound &mdash; gives Sancho&rsquo;s parents as <em>F&eacute;lix Lerena</em> and <em>Rosal&iacute;a
        Aguilar</em></strong>, citing <em>Revista 15</em> of the Instituto de Estudios Geneal&oacute;gicos
        del Uruguay. So <strong>Aguilar is his mother&rsquo;s surname</strong>, which is simply how Spanish
        naming works, and the Granada couple are not his parents.</p>
      <p>A full-text search agrees from the other side: <em>&ldquo;Juan de Lerena y Aguilar&rdquo;</em>
        returns <strong>five</strong> Madrid notarial hits, so that couple&rsquo;s sons do appear under the
        compound &mdash; and <em>&ldquo;Sancho de Lerena y Aguilar&rdquo;</em> returns
        <strong>No Results Found</strong>. There is no Sancho among them.</p>
      <div class="note" style="margin-top:14px"><strong>That caution stood for one day.</strong> It read:
        the pages of F&eacute;lix and Rosal&iacute;a carry no dates, no places and no other children, only
        the line <em>&ldquo;Family: Revista 15 IEGU&rdquo;</em>, so the parentage that retired the Granada
        reading rests on a single article nobody here has read &mdash; better than a naming-rule inference,
        and not an act. <strong>On 15 September 2026 it became an act.</strong></div>
      <p style="margin-top:16px"><strong>Iglesia Matriz, Montevideo, 10 February 1811.</strong> The baptism
        of Sancho&rsquo;s son, found by a full-text search for the phrase <em>&ldquo;Sancho Llerena&rdquo;</em>
        &mdash; a search this archive had run before, returning a result it never opened:</p>
      <blockquote style="margin:12px 0;padding-left:14px;border-left:3px solid var(--rule, #ccc)">
        <em>&ldquo;bautic&eacute; solemnemente a <strong>Lu&iacute;s Escol&aacute;stico</strong> que naci&oacute;
        ayer, hijo leg&iacute;timo de Don <strong>Sancho Llerena natural de Andaluc&iacute;a</strong> y de
        Do&ntilde;a <strong>Mar&iacute;a Fern&aacute;ndez natural de Asturias</strong>. Abuelos paternos
        <strong>Feliz [L]erena y Rosal&iacute;a Aguilar</strong>, &iacute;dem maternos <strong>Rodrigo
        Fern&aacute;ndez y Rosa Fern&aacute;ndez</strong>.&rdquo;</em>
      </blockquote>
      <p>Four things move at once. <strong>Sancho&rsquo;s parents</strong> are on a parish act written while
        he was alive and standing at his own son&rsquo;s font &mdash; <em>Feliz Gerena</em> being the
        machine&rsquo;s <strong>G-for-L</strong>, a seventh spelling for this family, and the same mangling
        behind the still-unresolved <em>&ldquo;Do&ntilde;a Andugara Gerena&rdquo;</em> in the Santa Rosa
        confirmations. <strong>Andaluc&iacute;a</strong> is no longer only on the census. <strong>Mar&iacute;a
        Fern&aacute;ndez is <em>natural de Asturias</em></strong>, which confirms the Operativo Patagonia
        reading from a Uruguayan document, and her parents <strong>Rodrigo and Rosa Fern&aacute;ndez</strong>
        are the couple the 1812 padr&oacute;n puts living in Sancho&rsquo;s household. And it is
        <strong>Luis&rsquo;s own baptism</strong>: born <strong>9 February 1811</strong>, baptised the next
        day, with a second forename &mdash; <em>Escol&aacute;stico</em> &mdash; nothing here had.</p>
      <p class="cap"><strong>What it does not give.</strong> C&oacute;rdoba is still on no act; it remains
        what the trees say, citing Apolant. And F&eacute;lix and Rosal&iacute;a themselves are still blank
        &mdash; no dates, no marriage, no other children. <strong>The parentage is documented; the people are
        not.</strong> The search that follows is F&eacute;lix Lerena and Rosal&iacute;a Aguilar in the
        C&oacute;rdoba parish registers, and it has not been run.</p>
      <p style="margin-top:14px"><strong>The same note separates two things this archive had welded
        together.</strong> It gives the marriage as <em>&ldquo;c.m. 8.1.1791&rdquo;</em> citing Apolant,
        <em>Operativo Patagonia</em>, ficha 355 &mdash; a named published work with a numbered entry. But
        <strong>Canelones appears nowhere in it.</strong> The phrase that follows the date,
        <em>&ldquo;Concejo de Morc&iacute;n, Asturias&rdquo;</em>, is the <em>bride&rsquo;s</em> birthplace:
        Josefa Fern&aacute;ndez Tresguerras, born 1778 in Asturias, of the <strong>Operativo
        Patagonia</strong> convoy &mdash; which is also why <em>Tresguerras</em> sits in no Uruguayan index,
        a question this archive raised and could not answer. <strong>The date has a source. The venue has
        none.</strong></p>
      <p>The venue is still the best inference available, and now for a better reason than habit.
        <strong>The Montevideo padr&oacute;n of 1812</strong> lists the household at <em>calle San Juan
        N&ordm; 60</em>: Sancho, <em>andaluz</em>, 45; Mar&iacute;a Fern&aacute;ndez, 36; his father-in-law
        <strong>Rodrigo Fern&aacute;ndez, 63, asturiano</strong>, living with them; <strong>four enslaved
        people</strong>; and six children <strong>all <em>&ldquo;naturales del Canel&oacute;n&rdquo;</em></strong>
        &mdash; Juan Agust&iacute;n 21, Pedro Antonio 19, Agustina 17, Fidel 12, Justa 10, Dominga 7. The
        eldest was born about 1791, in the town.</p>
      <p class="lab" style="margin-top:20px">15 September 2026 &mdash; and a branch at the other end of the
        city</p>
      <p>Searching <em>Fidel</em> rather than the surname turned up a Montevideo death registration of
        <strong>1889</strong>: <em>&ldquo;ha fallecido <strong>V&iacute;ctor Lerena</strong> de nacionalidad
        oriental de veinte y ocho a&ntilde;os de edad, de estado soltero y de profesi&oacute;n
        <strong>alpargatero</strong>, a consecuencia de tuberculosis pulmonar &hellip; el finado era hijo
        [de] <strong>Pedro Lorena</strong> y Josefa Seguera, orientales, finados. <strong>Abuelos paternos
        Fidel Lerena y Mar&iacute;a Ruedas</strong>.&rdquo;</em></p>
      <p>Three generations this archive had never seen, and the declaration was made <em>de oficio</em>
        &mdash; by the authorities, not by family. He died in hospital at twenty-eight, single, of
        tuberculosis. An <em>alpargatero</em> makes rope-soled shoes. <strong>Every other Lerena this
        archive has placed in Montevideo is a <em>hacendado</em>, a <em>juez de paz</em>, a major, or a
        senator&rsquo;s household.</strong> This is the same surname at the other end of that city.</p>
      <div class="note" style="margin-top:14px"><strong>Whether this Fidel is Sancho&rsquo;s son is not
        established.</strong> Sancho&rsquo;s Fidel was born about 1799 and the trees give him a death in
        1822 at twenty-two. For this to be one man, Pedro would have to be born by 1822 and fathering
        V&iacute;ctor at about forty &mdash; tight but possible, and that 1822 is tree-only and has never
        been tested against a Montevideo burial. The alternative is a second Fidel Lerena, which nothing
        rules out. <strong>Fidel is a rare forename here</strong>, and this archive worked out only
        yesterday where it came from: <em>Don Fidel Rey</em>, godfather at Agustina&rsquo;s baptism in 1795.
        The test is cheap and unrun &mdash; a Montevideo marriage of Fidel Lerena to Mar&iacute;a Ruedas.</div>
      <p class="lab" style="margin-top:20px">15 September 2026 &mdash; spelling the name a different way,
        and what fell out</p>
      <p>Every full-text sweep here had been run on <em>Sancho <strong>Ll</strong>erena</em>. Nobody had
        searched <em>Sancho <strong>L</strong>erena</em>, or <em>Sancho <strong>Lo</strong>rena</em>. Those
        two spellings return <strong>five Canelones and Montevideo documents</strong> that were not among
        the eighteen already worked through, and the surname has <strong>seven</strong> documented forms.</p>
      <p><strong>An eighth child.</strong> Canelones, 11 December 1806: <em>&ldquo;puso por nombre
        <strong>Jos&eacute; Loreto</strong>, hijo leg&iacute;timo de Sancho Lorena y de Mar&iacute;a
        Fern&aacute;ndez&rdquo;</em>, born the day before. He is in no tree and no census &mdash; and he is
        <strong>not in the 1812 padr&oacute;n</strong>, which lists six children and no boy of about five.
        The likeliest reading is that he died in between, which is usually what a household return&rsquo;s
        silence means about a small child. The tree&rsquo;s own prose says there were eight while naming
        seven; he may be the eighth.</p>
      <div class="note" style="margin-top:16px"><strong>And the four enslaved people in that household now
        have two names.</strong> The padr&oacute;n counts them and names none. Six weeks after it was taken,
        at the Catedral of Montevideo on <strong>26 September 1812</strong>:
        <em>&ldquo;bautic&eacute; solemnemente a <strong>Mar&iacute;a Mercedes</strong>, que naci&oacute;
        antes de ayer, <strong>negra esclava de Don Sancho Lerena y de Do&ntilde;a Mar&iacute;a
        Fern&aacute;ndez</strong>, hija de <strong>Juana</strong>. Madrina Catalina Mart&iacute;nez.&rdquo;</em> <strong>Juana</strong> is a woman
        this family held in slavery, named here for the first time; her daughter <strong>Mar&iacute;a
        Mercedes</strong> was born into that slavery in their household on 24 September 1812, six weeks
        before the census counted her mother without naming her. Both are now in the register as people.
        Nothing is known of what became of either of them, and this archive would rather carry two names
        than a number.</div>
      <p style="margin-top:16px"><strong>Two more things from a grandson&rsquo;s baptism.</strong> At the
        Catedral on <strong>22 March 1826</strong>, for a son of Dominga and Captain Antonio Rodr&iacute;guez
        de Carballo, the godfather is <em>&ldquo;el <strong>Mayor</strong> Don <strong>Pedro Antonio
        Llerena</strong>&rdquo;</em> &mdash; so Sancho&rsquo;s son held military rank at thirty-two, where his
        father had been a <em>soldado drag&oacute;n</em> in 1793. And the act writes the grandmother
        <em>&ldquo;Mar&iacute;a Fern&aacute;ndez <strong>Tesguerza</strong>&rdquo;</em>, which puts
        <strong>Tresguerras on a second act</strong> &mdash; this archive had it once, from 1830, and called
        that a vindication of the trees.</p>
      <p><strong>Three of those children are new to this archive</strong> and are now in the register:
        <strong>Juan Agust&iacute;n</strong> (b. c.1791), <strong>Agustina</strong> (b. c.1795) and
        <strong>Fidel</strong> (c.1799&ndash;1822). The first two settle an objection this archive raised
        against itself: at a Montevideo baptism of <strong>15 May 1812</strong> the godparents are written
        <em>&ldquo;Don Juan Agust&iacute;n <strong>Serena</strong> y Do&ntilde;a Agustina
        <strong>Perena</strong>&rdquo;</em>, and the note here read <em>&ldquo;Juan Agustin cannot be
        Sancho&rsquo;s son, who would be a child in 1812&rdquo;</em>. <strong>He was twenty-one and she was
        seventeen.</strong> The arithmetic was never checked, and <em>Serena</em> is the machine&rsquo;s
        S-for-L on this family for the sixth time.</p>
      <p><strong>And this one no longer rests on inference.</strong> FamilySearch&rsquo;s own
        <em>image index</em> for that page &mdash; a separate reading from the AI full-text transcript
        &mdash; lists the two godparents as <strong>&ldquo;Juan Agust&iacute;n Lerena&rdquo;</strong> and
        <strong>&ldquo;Agustina Alerena&rdquo;</strong>. Two pipelines disagree about the same ink, and the
        index reads <strong>L</strong>. So the names, the ages and an independent transcription now point
        the same way. <em>This archive still has not seen the handwriting</em> &mdash; the viewer&rsquo;s
        index panel would not dismiss &mdash; and that is the difference between this and an act.</p>
      <p class="cap">The same index names the maternal grandparents at that font as <strong>Rodrigo
        Fern&aacute;ndez and Rosa Fern&aacute;ndez</strong>, which is exactly the couple the trees give as
        <strong>Josefa Fern&aacute;ndez Tresguerras&rsquo;s parents</strong>. So the mother, Micaela, was
        Josefa&rsquo;s sister and the two Lerena teenagers at the font were the baby&rsquo;s first cousins
        &mdash; which is why they were there at all. This archive called that relationship &ldquo;a
        plausibility, not a finding&rdquo;; it now has an index behind it as well as a tree, and the
        padr&oacute;n puts Rodrigo Fern&aacute;ndez, 63, <em>asturiano</em>, living in Sancho&rsquo;s
        house.</p>
      <p class="cap">Note also what the census catches that the tree does not: <strong>Dominga</strong> is
        in the padr&oacute;n, and in a Canelones act of 1804, and <strong>missing from the tree&rsquo;s list
        of children</strong> &mdash; whose own prose says there were eight while naming seven. The tree is
        well sourced and incomplete, and the acts read here have already caught one of its gaps.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="panel" style="border-left:3px solid var(--accent);margin:22px 0">
    <p class="lab" style="margin:0 0 8px">14 September 2026 &mdash; and the family turns out not to have
      been obscure at all</p>
    <div class="prose">
      <p>Everything above treats this as a question about a <em>name</em>. The record below is about a
        <em>household</em>, and it was found by full-text search reading the handwriting itself &mdash;
        no search on <em>Lerena</em> would ever have returned it, because the surname is only in the
        grandparents&rsquo; line.</p>
      <p><strong>Nuestra Se&ntilde;ora del Carmen la Mayor, the Cord&oacute;n, outside Montevideo,
        13 December 1831.</strong> The baptism of <strong>Juan Facundo Frutuoso</strong>, son of Manuel
        Zeferino Barros of Pernambuco and of <strong>Justa Pastora Lerena, <em>natural de
        Canelones</em></strong>:</p>
      <blockquote class="rec" style="margin:14px 0"><em>&hellip;Abuelos &hellip; Maternos <strong>Sancho
        Llerena y Mar&iacute;a Fern&aacute;ndez</strong> &hellip; Fueron Padrinos Don <strong>Fructuoso
        Rivera, Presidente de la Rep&uacute;blica</strong>, y su esposa Do&ntilde;a <strong>Bernardina
        Fragoso</strong></em></blockquote>
      <div class="note" style="margin-top:14px"><strong>The first constitutional President of Uruguay
        stood godfather to Sancho&rsquo;s grandson</strong>, with the first lady beside him, and the
        child was named <em>Frutuoso</em> for him. Rivera held office 1830&ndash;1834; the act falls
        squarely in his first term.</div>
      <p style="margin-top:14px"><strong>Godparents of that rank are not chosen at random.</strong> This
        archive has carried the Lerenas as obscure &mdash; <em>naturales del E. Oriental</em> in a
        Rosario act, and very little else. The picture that has emerged since runs the other way at
        every point: <strong>Justiniana&rsquo;s father was a General</strong>; <strong>C&aacute;ndido
        Juanic&oacute; was an <em>abogado</em></strong>; <strong>Luis Eugenio died a
        <em>hacendado</em></strong>; Ema Sixta married the philosopher <strong>Jos&eacute; Pedro
        Massera</strong>; C&aacute;ndido Jaime married a <strong>Magari&ntilde;os</strong>. The 1831 act
        is the earliest point on that line and the sharpest.</p>
      <p><strong>It also produces a sister.</strong> <em>Justa Pastora Lerena</em>, born at Canelones, a
        daughter of Sancho and Mar&iacute;a Fern&aacute;ndez, married to a Brazilian &mdash; a person
        this archive had never met. Two more appear as godparents at her second child&rsquo;s font in
        1833: <strong>Avelino</strong> and <strong>Dominga Lerena</strong>.</p>
      <p class="cap"><strong>Said carefully: none of this touches Pablo Armando or Juan Carlos.</strong>
        It is the generation of Sancho&rsquo;s children, sixty years before them, and it is recorded as
        standing and as new siblings &mdash; not as descent.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="panel" style="margin:22px 0">
    <p class="lab" style="margin:0 0 8px">14 September 2026 &mdash; Sancho in 1789, and the tally counted properly</p>
    <div class="prose">
      <p>Yesterday this archive could not reach Sancho before <strong>1804</strong>. It can now reach him
        in <strong>1789</strong>. At the Iglesia Matriz de Montevideo on 30 June that year, the godparents
        of <em>Juana</em> &mdash; daughter of <strong>Nicol&aacute;s Benteno, <em>indio natural de la
        Ciudad del Tucum&aacute;n</em></strong>, and Leonarda Correa of Buenos Aires &mdash; were
        <strong>Sancho Llerena</strong> and Juliana Josefa Cornejo.</p>
      <p>He is about <strong>twenty-two</strong>, he is in <strong>Montevideo</strong> rather than
        Canelones, and he is two years short of the marriage his tree places at Canelones in 1791.
        <strong>It is the second time this archive has found him sponsoring at the margins of colonial
        society</strong> &mdash; in 1804 he stood godfather to an enslaved man, here to the daughter of
        an indigenous man from Tucum&aacute;n. Twice, fifteen years apart. That is a habit, not a
        coincidence, and it is recorded as it stands.</p>
      <p><strong>And the Sancho&ndash;or&ndash;Francisco tally has been counted wrongly, by this
        archive.</strong> Fourteen records now name him: <strong>ten say Sancho, four say
        Francisco</strong>. But counting records is the wrong arithmetic. <strong>Three of the four
        Franciscos are the same page</strong> &mdash; La Mauricia, 7 January 1846, one clerk writing
        three acts on one morning out of one bundle. They are one witness, not three.</p>
      <p>The Sanchos come from <strong>nine separate occasions across sixty-one years</strong>, in four
        parishes and two towns, and twice with the man himself standing there. <strong>So the evidence is
        not ten to four. It is nine independent occasions against two</strong> &mdash; and both of the
        two describe him at second hand, in the generation after his, by clerks who never met him.</p>
      <p class="cap"><strong>A double forename remains the leading reading</strong> and explains every
        record without anybody being wrong. What has changed is that this archive can no longer call the
        question evenly balanced. It had been treating one page as three votes.
        <strong>&ldquo;Aguilar&rdquo; is still unattested across all fourteen.</strong></p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="panel" style="margin:22px 0">
    <p class="lab" style="margin:0 0 8px">15 September 2026 &mdash; Sancho could not write, and the
      <em>informaci&oacute;n</em> that would have named his parents does not exist</p>
    <div class="prose">
      <p>The <em>informaciones matrimoniales</em> of Canelones were the best remaining hope for his
        parents. A pre-marriage investigation makes a Spanish groom state, on oath, who his parents were,
        where he was born, and how long he had been in the country &mdash; an independent second witness to
        everything the 1811 baptism says. This archive already knew Sancho was inside that series, because
        a full-text sweep had found him there once.</p>
      <p><strong>He is there as a witness, and the document has now been read in full.</strong> At
        <strong>image 2167</strong> of DGS 8166573, on <strong>9 January 1798</strong>, the margin reads
        <em>&ldquo;2.<sup>do</sup> Sancho Llerena&rdquo;</em> and the text runs: <em>&ldquo;Seguidamente
        Continuando dicha informaci&oacute;n, Florentino G&oacute;mez present&oacute; por testigo en esta
        Vicar&iacute;a a <strong>Sancho Llerena</strong>&hellip; dixo: que Con motivo de haver
        <strong>andado el declarante en la L&iacute;nea de Vicar&iacute;a algunos a&ntilde;os</strong>, que
        Conoci&oacute; en aquel parage a Florentino G&oacute;mez&hellip;&rdquo;</em> He is the
        <strong>second of the two witnesses</strong>, not the groom &mdash; a thing this archive had left
        open.</p>
      <blockquote class="rec" style="margin:14px 0"><em>&hellip;y no la firm&oacute;
        <strong>porque dixo no saver escrivir</strong>, y la firm&oacute; su Merced Conmigo el presente
        Notario que de ello doy fee.</em></blockquote>
      <p><strong>Sancho Llerena could not write.</strong> It is the first fact this archive holds about the
        man rather than about his household, and it comes off a parish document in his own presence. It
        also closes a door: <strong>no signature of his survives to compare against anything.</strong> And
        it sits pointedly beside the 1811 baptism of his son Luis, which calls him <em>&ldquo;Don Sancho
        Llerena&rdquo;</em>. <strong>An illiterate <em>Don</em> is a man whose standing came from something
        other than schooling</strong> &mdash; which fits a soldier, and fits the two occasions on which he
        stood godfather at the margins of colonial society.</p>
      <p class="lab" style="margin-top:16px">And the expediente we wanted is not in the series</p>
      <p>The reel was walked by image number. Its filming targets cut it into volumes:
        <strong>Volumen 6 runs 1776 to November 1790</strong> and ends at image 1459;
        <strong>Volumen 7 opens at image 1460</strong>, and its first dated expediente is
        <strong>March 1791</strong>. <strong>Sancho married on 8 January 1791.</strong> The entire 1791
        content of this series is one expediente belonging to somebody else &mdash; <em>Juan Manuel
        S&aacute;nchez</em>, an Andalusian, images 1462&ndash;1464 &mdash; and by 1464 the archivist&rsquo;s
        pencil already reads <em>&ldquo;Marzo 1792&rdquo;</em>.</p>
      <p><strong>January 1791 falls in the seam between two volumes.</strong> That is a gap in the filing,
        not a failure to look, and the difference matters: the two are indistinguishable from a distance and
        only one of them is worth returning to. The honest caveat is that the filming card at image 1461
        reads <strong>&ldquo;HOJAS SUELTAS&rdquo;</strong> &mdash; loose leaves &mdash; so the order is
        whatever order somebody filed them in, and a stray 1791 sheet elsewhere in Volumen 7 is not
        impossible. It is simply not where it should be.</p>
      <div class="note"><strong>Which is why the castrense books now go first.</strong> The 1793 Canelones
        baptism of his son Pedro Antonio calls Sancho <em>&ldquo;el soldado drag&oacute;n&rdquo;</em> and
        was performed by the chaplain of the <strong>Regimiento de Infanter&iacute;a de Buenos Ayres</strong>.
        A serving soldier&rsquo;s marriage needed his commander&rsquo;s licence and frequently ran through
        the military chaplaincy rather than the parish <em>vicar&iacute;a</em>. That would explain, without
        any appeal to loss or accident, <strong>why he appears in the vicar&iacute;a&rsquo;s
        <em>informaciones</em> as a witness but never as a petitioner</strong>.</div>
      <div class="note" style="margin-top:12px"><strong>That argument has since been weakened by the
        film itself, and the weakening is recorded here rather than quietly dropped.</strong> DGS 7713373
        was opened on 15 September 2026. The regiment&rsquo;s book is real and covers 1791 &mdash; but at
        <strong>image 660</strong>, on <strong>30 January 1791</strong>, it baptises the son of
        <em>&ldquo;<strong>Manuel S&aacute;nchez, Sargento</strong> de dicho Regimiento&rdquo;</em>. And the
        single 1791 expediente in the entire Canelones <em>informaciones</em> belongs to a
        <em>&ldquo;<strong>Juan Manuel S&aacute;nchez</strong>, natural del Reino de Andaluc&iacute;a&rdquo;</em>,
        marrying that March. <strong>If those are one man, a soldier of this very regiment did go through
        the Canelones vicar&iacute;a and did leave an <em>informaci&oacute;n</em> there</strong> &mdash; which
        cuts against the explanation above rather than supporting it. It is <strong>not established</strong>:
        S&aacute;nchez is a common name and the castrense entry&rsquo;s birthplace runs off the page edge.
        It is written down because it tells against the reading this archive would prefer.
        <strong style="display:block;margin-top:10px">Settled later the same day &mdash; against the
        objection.</strong> The page edge was read at magnification: <em>&ldquo;hijo leg&iacute;timo del
        <strong>Manuel S&aacute;nchez, Sargento de 1&ordf; Clase del Regimiento, natural de la Ciudad de
        C&oacute;rdova</strong>&rdquo;</em>. The Canelones petitioner is <em>&ldquo;natural del
        <strong>Reino de Andaluc&iacute;a</strong>&rdquo;</em>. <strong>The two descriptions do not
        match.</strong> A man from the Andalusian city would be called <em>of Andaluc&iacute;a</em> &mdash;
        which is what the Canelones clerk writes, and what Sancho&rsquo;s own act means by <em>C&oacute;rdova
        la Llana</em> &mdash; while an unqualified <em>la Ciudad de C&oacute;rdova</em> in a Montevideo
        register most naturally means <strong>C&oacute;rdoba del Tucum&aacute;n</strong>, which this same
        book names in full a few leaves earlier. <strong>So the objection is withdrawn</strong> &mdash; and
        removing a counter-example is not producing evidence. The castrense explanation is un-attacked, not
        proved.</div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="panel" style="margin:22px 0">
    <p class="lab" style="margin:0 0 8px">And a published tree turns out to have been right about something no index holds</p>
    <div class="prose">
      <p>The marriage act of <strong>3 November 1830</strong> has now been read rather than indexed:
        <em>&ldquo;&hellip; con <strong>Justa Pastora Llerena, natural de Canelones, residente en el
        Cord&oacute;n</strong>, hija leg&iacute;tima de Sancho Llerena y de <strong>Mar&iacute;a
        Fern&aacute;ndez Tresguerras</strong>.&rdquo;</em></p>
      <p><strong>Tresguerras.</strong> This archive has carried <em>Mar&iacute;a Josefa Fern&aacute;ndez
        Tresguerras</em> since its first week, taken from a published tree, marked <strong>secondary</strong>
        and unverified &mdash; the matronymic appears in no index anywhere. <strong>The tree was right.</strong></p>
      <p>That matters beyond one name. The same tree carries an unopened claim &mdash; that Sancho and
        Mar&iacute;a married at <strong>Canelones on 8 January 1791</strong> &mdash; and its credit has
        just gone up. <strong>That act would settle the forename, and it has still never been opened.</strong></p>
      <p class="cap">She is also the one fixed point in her husband&rsquo;s identity: across fourteen
        records his forename alternates and <strong>every single one of them names her</strong>.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="panel" style="margin:22px 0">
    <p class="lab" style="margin:0 0 8px">A caution answered the same afternoon it was written</p>
    <div class="prose">
      <p>Earlier today this archive recorded that two records name a <em>Dominga</em>, daughter of Sancho
        and Mar&iacute;a Fern&aacute;ndez, with <strong>two different husbands</strong> &mdash; and
        refused to merge them. One of the two images has now been opened.</p>
      <p>The Catedral act of <strong>12 May 1827</strong>: <em>&ldquo;Lucinda Gregoria del Coraz&oacute;n
        de Jes&uacute;s &hellip; hija leg&iacute;tima del <strong>Capit&aacute;n Don Antonio Ru&iacute;z
        de Carvallo</strong> y de <strong>Do&ntilde;a Dominga Llerena de Carvallo</strong>. Nieta Paterna
        del <strong>Mayor Don Joaqu&iacute;n Ru&iacute;z de Carvallo</strong> &hellip; Materna de
        <strong>Sancho Llerena y de Do&ntilde;a Mar&iacute;a Fern&aacute;ndez</strong>.&rdquo;</em></p>
      <p>So Sancho&rsquo;s daughter married a captain, son of a major &mdash; and the godfather was the
        <strong>Excelent&iacute;simo Se&ntilde;or General Don Francisco de Paula Maggesi</strong>. A
        second general at a Lerena font.</p>
      <p><strong>And it identifies a woman this archive had on its register and could not place.</strong>
        The <em>&ldquo;Do&ntilde;a Dominga Carvallo&rdquo;</em> who stood godmother at Alberto
        Octavio&rsquo;s baptism in 1850 is almost certainly this one &mdash; a Lerena by birth, standing
        for her own brother&rsquo;s son, not an outsider.</p>
      <p class="cap"><strong>Not merged, and the caution is not withdrawn.</strong> The 1850 act gives her
        only as <em>Carvallo</em>, and the other Dominga record &mdash; the one naming a husband
        <em>Antonio Rodr&iacute;guez</em> and a son <em>Domingo</em> &mdash; is still unopened. One woman
        twice married, or a sister of the same name. Half an answer is recorded as half an answer.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="panel" style="margin:22px 0">
    <p class="lab" style="margin:0 0 8px">14 September 2026 &mdash; a page read end to end, and where the family stood in the war</p>
    <div class="prose">
      <p>This archive opened the baptism page of <strong>7 January 1846</strong> at the Capilla de la
        Mauricia a day earlier and read the Lerena entries off it. It did not read <em>the page</em>.
        Doing that corrected a branch and answered a question nobody had asked.</p>
      <p><strong>Every act on the page ends the same way</strong> &mdash; not the Lerena ones, all of
        them, the Peraltas and Quirogas and Oteros too:
        <em>&ldquo;as&iacute; consta del <strong>legajo</strong> conservado en este Archivo, mandado
        <strong>trasladar</strong> por el Se&ntilde;or Obispo y Vicario Apost&oacute;lico D. Jacinto
        Vera.&rdquo;</em> This is not a parish register being kept week by week. It is a bundle of loose
        papers being copied into a book, three years late, on a bishop&rsquo;s order.</p>
      <p><strong>The cause is the Guerra Grande.</strong> Oribe&rsquo;s army closed on Montevideo in
        <strong>February 1843</strong> and the siege ran nine years. The three Lerena children on this
        page were born in <strong>February, May and July 1843</strong> &mdash; the first of them in the
        month the siege began &mdash; and not one of them was christened until January 1846.</p>
      <p><strong>And where they were christened places the family.</strong> The Capilla de la Mauricia
        stood in the <strong>Villa de la Uni&oacute;n</strong>, which Oribe renamed the <strong>Villa
        Restauraci&oacute;n</strong> and made his seat &mdash; the government outside the walls, for the
        duration. The Lerenas were not inside the besieged city. They were in Oribe&rsquo;s town, having
        their children baptised by Oribe&rsquo;s priest.</p>
      <p><strong>Which joins up with two things this archive already held separately.</strong> Luis
        Lerena&rsquo;s first wife, married on 3 September 1836, was <strong>Carolina Oribe</strong>. And
        the 1850 baptism of his son Alberto Octavio is recorded at <em>S. Agust&iacute;n, pueblo de la
        <strong>Restauraci&oacute;n</strong></em> &mdash; the same place, under Oribe&rsquo;s name for it,
        four years later.</p>
      <p class="cap"><strong>Said as what it is.</strong> A family living in the Villa Restauraci&oacute;n
        through the siege, marrying an Oribe and baptising in Oribe&rsquo;s parish, was on the
        <strong>blanco</strong> side of that war. That is a reading of where they lived and whom they
        married &mdash; not a claim from any political record &mdash; and <strong>no relationship to
        Manuel Oribe is asserted</strong>. This archive has already had to withdraw one reading of that
        bride&rsquo;s surname.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="panel" style="margin:22px 0">
    <p class="lab" style="margin:0 0 8px">The same page, and a man given to the wrong brother</p>
    <div class="prose">
      <p>The third Lerena entry reads:
        <em>&ldquo;bautiz&oacute; solemnemente a <strong>Alejandro</strong>, que naci&oacute; el veinte y
        nueve de Mayo &hellip; hijo leg&iacute;timo de <strong>Avelino Serena y Ambrosia Traibel</strong>
        &hellip; Padrinos <strong>Lu&iacute;s [L]erena y Justiniana Lenguas</strong>.&rdquo;</em></p>
      <p>This archive published him on 13 September as <strong>&ldquo;a sixth child of Luis Lerena and
        Justiniana Lenguas&rdquo;</strong>. He is <strong>Avelino&rsquo;s</strong> son. Luis and
        Justiniana are his <em>godparents</em>, and the two names sit in the same sentence as the parents
        &mdash; the archive took the wrong pair out of it.</p>
      <p>Correcting it gains more than it costs. <strong>Alejandro is the eldest known child of Avelino
        and Ambrosia Traibel</strong> &mdash; eight years before Josefa, fifteen before Alfredo &mdash;
        which pushes their marriage back before May 1843. And the act shows the <strong>two brothers
        bringing their children to the same font on the same morning and standing for each
        other&rsquo;s</strong>.</p>
      <p class="cap">He is also a fourth <em>Alejandro</em>, against three already on the
        <a href={u("/register/")}>register</a>. Nothing links him to any of them, and they stay
        separate rows.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="panel" style="margin:22px 0">
    <p class="lab" style="margin:0 0 8px">And a date this archive had to overrule a document to fix</p>
    <div class="prose">
      <p>Luis Eugenio&rsquo;s act says he was born <strong>9 February 1843</strong>. The entry
        immediately above it, in the same hand, says his sister <strong>Justiniana Mauricia M&aacute;xima
        was born 30 July 1843</strong>. <strong>Five and a half months, between two full siblings.</strong>
        Both cannot be true, and neither reading is a machine&rsquo;s &mdash; each act has been read
        word by word.</p>
      <p>Rather than choose between them, the archive went looking for a third record. <strong>His civil
        death registration of 24 December 1901 gives his age as fifty-six</strong> &mdash; a birth of
        about <strong>1845</strong>. So two independent constraints point at 1845 and one points at 1843,
        and the one is a clerk copying loose papers who had written <em>&ldquo;a&ntilde;o cuarenta y
        tres&rdquo;</em> in the entry above this one and wrote it again in the entry below.
        <strong>A year carried down a column is the ordinary way this error happens.</strong></p>
      <p class="cap">The register now reads <strong>9 February 1845</strong>; the day and month were never
        in question. The act&rsquo;s own words are kept, because the source says 1843 and a reader is
        entitled to see that this archive overruled it, and why. <strong>An age at death is the weakest
        date in genealogy</strong> &mdash; it carries weight here only because it agrees with a
        constraint reached without it.</p>
      <p class="cap"><strong>None of this would have surfaced but for a broken guard.</strong> The
        archive&rsquo;s consistency checker was written for exactly this pair of dates and had been
        printing <em>&ldquo;nothing contradicts itself&rdquo;</em> for a week &mdash; because it could
        only see the <strong>thirty of two hundred and forty-five</strong> people who carry a structured
        parent field. It now prints its own coverage.
        <a href={u("/corrections/")}>Both corrections &rarr;</a></p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="panel" style="margin:22px 0">
    <p class="lab" style="margin:0 0 8px">14 September 2026 &mdash; the apex generation, found by searching the parents instead of the name</p>
    <div class="prose">
      <p>Every search this archive ran for two weeks began with the surname. That surname is written
        <em>Lerena, Llerena, Lorena, Serena, Lerina, Sancio, Gerena</em> and <em>Levena</em> across
        these registers &mdash; eight spellings &mdash; so the surname box was losing records the whole
        time. <strong>Searching instead for the couple</strong> &mdash; father <em>Sancho</em>, mother
        <em>Mar&iacute;a Fern&aacute;ndez</em> &mdash; returned twenty-two results and four things this
        archive did not have.</p>
      <p><strong>Avelino now has dates: born 1815, died 22 August 1890 at Montevideo</strong>,
        registered the following day, the act naming his parents as Sancho and Mar&iacute;a
        Fern&aacute;ndez. That is an independent attestation of a parentage the 1858 baptisms had
        already settled, and it is the first record here that says when Avelino lived.</p>
      <p><strong>It also closes something the archive had filed as merely odd.</strong> When his son
        Alfredo married at the Catedral in February 1892, the witness beside Carlos Navia was
        <em>Do&ntilde;a Ambrosia Traibel</em> &mdash; the groom&rsquo;s mother, where a father would be
        expected. Avelino had been dead sixteen months. Three records taken years apart now fall into
        one order.</p>
      <p><strong>And Justa Pastora&rsquo;s marriage is dated: 3 November 1830</strong>, at Nuestra
        Se&ntilde;ora del Carmen la Mayor in the Cord&oacute;n, to <em>Manuel Barros</em>, she
        <em>natural de Canelones</em>, her parents Sancho Llerena and Mar&iacute;a Fern&aacute;ndez.
        Which puts <strong>thirteen months</strong> between that wedding and the font, in the same
        parish, at which the President of the Republic stood godfather to her first child.</p>
      <p class="cap">Both of those are <strong>indexes read, images not yet opened</strong> &mdash;
        secondary for the dates, while the parentages stay primary on acts already read here.</p>
      <p><strong>The fourth finding is a warning rather than a fact.</strong> Two records name a
        <em>Dominga</em>, daughter of Sancho and Mar&iacute;a Fern&aacute;ndez, with <strong>two
        different husbands</strong>: <em>Antonio Rodr&iacute;guez</em>, with a son Domingo, and
        <em>Antonio Ru&iacute;z de Carvallo</em>, with a daughter Lucinda Gregoria. The image
        references are adjacent, so the two acts sit near each other in one register. That is one woman
        married twice, or one man whose name is written two ways, or two sisters sharing a forename
        &mdash; and <strong>this archive has been caught by that exact shape twice</strong>, with the
        two Arturos and the three Josefas. Both husbands being <em>Antonio</em> is suggestive and is
        not proof. <strong>Nothing has been merged.</strong> It is worth adding that a
        <em>Do&ntilde;a Dominga Carvallo</em> stood godmother at a Lerena font in 1850 &mdash; which
        makes the Carvallo record something to read rather than something to assume.</p>
      <p class="cap"><strong>The method is the finding.</strong> A family indexed under eight spellings
        is not reliably reachable by its name. It is reachable by the names of the people standing
        beside it &mdash; parents, spouses, godparents &mdash; and those are spelled ordinarily.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="panel" style="margin:22px 0">
    <p class="lab" style="margin:0 0 8px">And the other side of that generation, back two further steps</p>
    <div class="prose">
      <p>The death act of <strong>Sixta Lenguas</strong> &mdash; Justiniana&rsquo;s sister, and the second
        wife of C&aacute;ndido Juanic&oacute; &mdash; Montevideo, 17 January 1884, reaches further back
        than anything else this archive holds:</p>
      <blockquote class="rec" style="margin:14px 0"><em>hija de D. <strong>Pedro Lenguas</strong> y de
        D&ordf; <strong>Carmen Gonz&aacute;lez</strong>, finados; sus abuelos paternos D. <strong>Antonio
        Lenguas</strong> y D&ordf; <strong>Juana Grandal</strong>; maternos D. <strong>Pascual
        Gonz&aacute;lez</strong> y D&ordf; <strong>Josefa Grandal</strong></em></blockquote>
      <p>That is the <strong>great-great-grandparents of Luis Eugenio and Gilberto</strong>, named on one
        page. And an <strong>Antonio Lenguas born 1807 at BUENOS AIRES</strong>, buried 1841 at Las
        Piedras, Canelones, with a wife <em>Juana Grandal</em>, is already in the indexed Uruguayan
        records &mdash; almost certainly this man, which would make the Lenguas a Buenos Aires family
        before they were a Montevideo one.</p>
      <div class="note" style="margin-top:14px"><strong>Both grandmothers are Grandal.</strong> Juana on
        one side, Josefa on the other. <strong>Pedro Lenguas and Carmen Gonz&aacute;lez were very
        probably cousins.</strong> Recorded as an observation &mdash; no record read here links the two
        women directly.</div>
      <p class="cap" style="margin-top:14px">The same act gives C&aacute;ndido Juanic&oacute; as
        <em>&ldquo;Oriental, edad 72 a&ntilde;os, viudo, <strong>Abogado</strong>&rdquo;</em> &mdash; born
        about 1812, and a lawyer, which is what this archive&rsquo;s &ldquo;Dr.&rdquo; had always rested
        on without a document.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
      <p style="margin-top:14px"><strong>So the descent from Sancho is no longer user-submitted.</strong>
        It is written in three registers, in three hands, across fourteen years. The <em>rest</em> of
        what that paragraph describes &mdash; the 1767 birth at C&oacute;rdoba, the 1791 marriage at
        Canelones, the fourteen attached acts &mdash; is still <strong>secondary</strong> and still
        unopened. What changed is the join: <strong>Sancho to Luis, and so to everyone below him.</strong></p>
      <p style="margin-top:14px"><strong>The 1836 petition also opened a marriage nobody knew about</strong>
        &mdash; and the paragraph above this one, on the page for a day, is where the first wife was
        named <strong>Carolina <em>Oliva</em></strong>. <strong>Her surname is ORIBE.</strong></p>
      <div class="note"><strong>And this page had it right before I did.</strong> The Sancho paragraph
        higher up carries, from a <em>secondary</em> published tree, that Don Luis <em>&ldquo;married
        firstly a daughter of a <strong>Oribe</strong>&rdquo;</em>. FamilySearch&rsquo;s index of the 1836
        petition says <em>Oliva</em>; I read the image expecting Oliva and saw it, twice, and wrote it
        down as settled. A Catedral baptism of <strong>4 July 1837</strong> &mdash; another book, another
        hand &mdash; names <em>&ldquo;D. Luis Lerena y Dña <strong>Carolina Oribe</strong>&rdquo;</em>
        standing godparents to his brother&rsquo;s child. Re-opened and magnified, the petition reads
        <strong>Oribe</strong> too.</div>
      <p style="margin-top:14px">That act gives two more things. <strong>Another brother</strong> &mdash;
        <em>D. Pedro Antonio Lerena, natural de esta</em>, with the same <em>abuelos paternos D. Sancho y
        Dña María Fernández</em>, married to Vicenta Vega of Cerro Largo. And it proves
        <strong>Carolina was alive in July 1837</strong>: married September 1836, alive that July, dead
        by March 1842.</p>
      <p class="cap"><strong>ORIBE is one of the great Uruguayan names</strong> &mdash; Manuel Oribe was
        President 1835&ndash;1838 and founder of the Blancos. So within five years this family has
        <strong>Fructuoso Rivera</strong> at a font and an <strong>Oribe</strong> for a bride: the two
        men whose quarrel became the Guerra Grande. <strong>Nothing read here makes Carolina kin to the
        president.</strong> Oribe is a surname, not a proof, and no record yet names her parents. It is
        recorded as a striking fact about this family&rsquo;s connections and as a question to pursue.</p>
      <p class="cap"><strong>As for children of that marriage: none found, and this time the null is
        worth something.</strong> The Montevideo registers for 1837&ndash;41 <em>are</em> in the
        full-text corpus &mdash; Cordón baptisms 1837&ndash;41, Catedral baptisms 1836&ndash;40, Catedral
        deaths 1836&ndash;42 &mdash; so a machine has read the handwriting, and nothing returns.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
  <div class="panel" style="border-left:3px solid var(--ochre);margin:22px 0">
    <p class="lab" style="margin:0 0 8px">And the two spellings can be counted</p>
    <div class="prose">
      <p>FamilySearch&rsquo;s full-text corpus covers both Montevideo and Buenos Aires, and its Place
        filter gives per-country counts. Searching each surname as an exact phrase:</p>
    </div>
    <div class="scroll" style="margin-top:12px">
      <table class="rec">
        <tr><th></th><th>Argentina</th><th>Uruguay</th><th></th></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>LERENA</strong></td><td class="mono">701</td><td class="mono"><strong>4,517</strong></td>
          <td><strong>Uruguay 6.4&times; Argentina</strong></td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>LLERENA</strong></td><td class="mono"><strong>3,415</strong></td><td class="mono">947</td>
          <td>Argentina 3.6&times; Uruguay</td></tr>
        <tr><td><em>Fernández</em> (reference)</td><td class="mono">608,205</td><td class="mono">305,473</td>
          <td>Argentina 2.0&times; Uruguay</td></tr>
        <tr><td><em>Bustamante</em> (reference)</td><td class="mono">75,647</td><td class="mono">9,328</td>
          <td>Argentina 8.1&times; Uruguay</td></tr>
      </table>
    </div>
    <div class="prose" style="margin-top:14px">
      <p>A control is necessary because the two national corpora differ in size and every surname has its
        own geography &mdash; so the comparison is made against <strong>two reference surnames measured
        the same way</strong>, not against an assumed corpus ratio.</p>
      <p><strong>Both references run Argentina-heavy</strong>, one by twice and one by eight times.
        <strong>So does <em>Llerena</em>.</strong> <strong><em>Lerena</em> is the only one of the four
        that reverses</strong> &mdash; and it is the spelling this family used.</p>
      <p class="cap">What it does not say: this measures a <em>digitised corpus</em>, not a population.
        Coverage is uneven between countries and periods and the handwriting recognition misreads. It is
        evidence about where the <em>name</em> lives, not where any particular family lived, and it
        cannot place Juan Carlos Lerena anywhere.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
  <p><span class="chip inf">Split in two, 13 September &mdash; the River Plate half now points to Andalusia</span>
    <a href={u("/name/")}>The name &rarr;</a></p>
</section>
<section class="blk">
<h2 id="h-1b-that-the-family-was-italian-not-spanish">1b. That the family was ITALIAN, not Spanish</h2>
  <p><strong>That Juan Carlos or María Lerena was an Italian immigrant to Rosario, and the family&rsquo;s
    name is nearer LERINA than Lerena.</strong></p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>For:</strong> a granddaughter&rsquo;s DNA carries <strong>0% Iberian</strong> and
      <strong>11.1% Italian</strong>, northern-dominant. Her other three grandparents are English and Cape
      Afrikaner, so the Italian has nowhere else to come from; at one quarter it implies roughly
      <strong>44% in him</strong> &mdash; about one Italian parent. <a href={u("/dna/")}>The DNA &rarr;</a></li>
    <li><strong>For:</strong> it explains the <strong>missing marriage</strong>. A couple who married in
      Italy before emigrating would never appear in the Rosario register, which has now been read clean
      for 1867&ndash;1885.</li>
    <li><strong>For:</strong> his death was registered <strong>LERINA</strong> &mdash; treated here for a
      month as a clerk&rsquo;s slip. CEMLA records <strong>Lerina as an <em>Italian</em> surname</strong>,
      arriving from Genova. The &ldquo;error&rdquo; may be the one document that got it right.</li>
    <li><strong>For:</strong> the register around his birth, read act by act, is <strong>full of Ligurian
      and Piedmontese names</strong>. Rosario in 1882 was the most Italian city in Argentina.</li>
    <li><strong>Against:</strong> ethnicity estimates are not records. At one quarter the noise is large,
      and MyHeritage is known to distribute Iberian across French, North Italian and Sardinian. And every
      indexed <em>Lerena</em> arrival at CEMLA is <em>Española</em>; it is the <em>Lerina</em> arrivals who
      are <em>Italiana</em>.</li>
    <li><strong>To settle it:</strong> a DNA match with a documented Italian or Argentine tree, and a
      sweep of the Rosario indexes under <strong>LERINA</strong>, which was never separately looked for.</li>
    <li><strong>Against, and it arrived within the hour:</strong> at low confidence <strong>all eight
      genetic groups</strong> are shown, and <strong>no Italian group is among the five that are
      legible</strong> &mdash; they are two South African, two British Isles, and the Río de la Plata one.
      The easiest confirmation was available and did not appear. Three group names remain unseen.</li>
    <li><strong>Against, 13 September, and this one is heavier:</strong> the Rosario baptism index has
      now been read end to end, 1731 to 1901, and the <em>one</em> Lerena in it is a <strong>Uruguayan
      from the Montevideo patriciate</strong> &mdash; a Lerena Lenguas married to a Juanic&oacute;, with
      an Oribe in the generation above. The surname in the River Plate has a documented home, and it is
      not Liguria. <a href={u("/searched/")}>What was read &rarr;</a></li>
    <li><strong>Still standing, and not dismissed:</strong> the death certificate says
      <strong>LERINA</strong>, and nothing found today explains that. The four LERINA arrivals of 1889
      remain excluded &mdash; but on their dates, not on a judgement about the surname, which was
      <a href={u("/corrections/")}>corrected the same day</a>.</li>
  </ul>
  <p><span class="chip inf">New, and weaker than when it was written &mdash; twice over</span>
    <a href={u("/dna/")}>The DNA &rarr;</a></p>
</section>
<section class="blk">
<h2 id="h-1c-that-the-family-came-from-uruguay-not-argentina">1c. That the family came from URUGUAY, not Argentina</h2>
  <p><strong>That Juan Carlos Lerena was a Uruguayan who came to Rosario, and that the Lerenas of the
    River Plate are a Montevideo house rather than a Santa Fe one.</strong></p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>For, and it is new:</strong> the Rosario baptism index has now been read end to end,
      <strong>both volumes, 1731 to 1901</strong>. The surname LERENA occurs <strong>once</strong> in a
      hundred and seventy years. The act behind that one entry was opened the same day, and it says of
      both parents: <em><strong>&ldquo;naturales del E. Oriental&rdquo;</strong></em> &mdash; natives of
      the Estado Oriental del Uruguay. <a href={u("/searched/")}>The sweep &rarr;</a></li>
    <li><strong>For:</strong> the mother in that act is a <strong>Juanicó</strong>, and the father a
      <strong>Lerena Lenguas</strong>. Both are Montevideo names, not Santa Fe ones.</li>
    <li><strong>For:</strong> the godfather &mdash; another <strong>Don Luis Lerena</strong> &mdash; did
      not attend. He was <strong>represented by proxy</strong>. Godparents stand by proxy when they are
      somewhere else, and for this family the somewhere else is across the river.</li>
    <li><strong>For, by explaining four separate negatives at once:</strong> no Rosario baptism for Pablo
      Armando; none for Juan Carlos; no Lerena marriage at Rosario, 1867&ndash;1885; and no Lerena in the
      parish index at all but for one Uruguayan. A family that lived in a parish leaves baptisms. This
      one leaves nothing &mdash; because it was not there.</li>
    <li><strong>Against:</strong> <strong>no link has been shown</strong> between Luis Lerena Lenguas and
      Juan Carlos Lerena. Two men sharing a surname in one city sixteen years apart is a lead, not a
      relation. The 1866 child is emphatically <em>not</em> Pablo Armando&rsquo;s sister.</li>
    <li><strong>Against:</strong> Pablo Armando himself was <strong>born at Rosario</strong> &mdash;
      documented twice over, on his passport and his enrolment card. Uruguayan origin for his father is
      not Uruguayan birth for him.</li>
    <li><strong>Where the DNA sits:</strong> awkwardly, and honestly so. The Río de la Plata genetic
      group that can only come from him <strong>spans both banks</strong>. It cannot tell Buenos Aires
      from Montevideo, and never could. What it does say is that the Argentine and the Uruguayan
      hypotheses are, genetically, <em>the same hypothesis</em>. <a href={u("/dna/")}>The DNA &rarr;</a></li>
    <li><strong>To settle it:</strong> <em>Uruguay, Registros Parroquiales, 1750&ndash;1930</em> &mdash;
      a baptism for <strong>Juan Carlos Lerena</strong> in the 1840s or 1850s, and a marriage to a
      <strong>María</strong> before 1882. Then Luis Lerena Lenguas and Julia Juanicó in Montevideo, whose
      own marriage would fix the generation above.</li>
  </ul>
  <div class="panel" style="border-left:3px solid var(--ochre);margin:22px 0">
    <p class="lab" style="margin:0 0 8px">The same evening &mdash; two routes meet</p>
    <div class="prose">
      <p>The act names the father <strong>Luis Lerena Lenguas</strong> and the proxy godfather
        <strong>Don Luis Lerena</strong>. That pair is enough to find the household in published
        Uruguayan genealogy: <strong>Luis Lerena y Fern&aacute;ndez (1811&ndash;1878)</strong>, widower
        of a <strong>Oribe</strong>, married secondly <strong>Justiniana Lenguas Gonz&aacute;lez</strong>,
        daughter of Gen. Pedro Lenguas. Their children carry the compound. The Montevideo patriciate.</p>
      <p>Two things follow. The <strong>proxy godfather gets a name</strong> &mdash; the child&rsquo;s
        paternal grandfather, in Montevideo &mdash; and the child&rsquo;s <strong>fifth given name is
        <em>Justiniana</em></strong>, for that grandmother. The act and the genealogy describe one
        family, and neither was written with the other in view.</p>
      <p>And <strong>one of his sons is already in this archive</strong>.
        <strong>Gilberto Justiniano Lerena Lenguas</strong>, Montevideo 1855 &ndash; 1912:
        <strong>co-founder of the Argentine Stud Book</strong>, breeder of the Quadruple Crown winner
        <em>Old Man</em>, the man for whom a Group 1 at Palermo was <strong>renamed in 1914</strong>. He
        has sat in this register since the 1895 Buenos Aires census, marked <em>high interest</em>, for a
        reason with nothing to do with Uruguay &mdash; <strong>Pablo Armando Lerena trained and owned
        racehorses</strong>.</p>
      <p>That was always the loudest coincidence in the file, and it was set aside <em>as</em> a
        coincidence. It still is one. But the parish register of Rosario and the turf of Buenos Aires now
        arrive at the same Montevideo house from opposite ends, by methods sharing no assumption.
        <a href={u("/horses/")}>The horses &rarr;</a></p>
      <p><strong>The evidence tiers matter here.</strong> The act is primary and was read at full
        resolution. Everything about the generations above it is <strong>secondary</strong> &mdash;
        Geneanet, Geni, a genealogical blog &mdash; none citing a checkable source. They agree with each
        other and with a document they were not written from. That is the best that can be said until the
        Montevideo registers are opened, and it is not proof.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
  <div class="panel" style="border-left:3px solid var(--accent);margin:22px 0">
    <p class="lab" style="margin:0 0 8px">13 September 2026 &mdash; the Montevideo registers were opened,
      which is what the paragraph above said it was waiting for</p>
    <div class="prose">
      <p>They were opened, and they hold. <strong>The household is now documented from the registers
        rather than from the blogs</strong>, and the two claims that mattered most here are the two that
        moved.</p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Gilberto&rsquo;s parents are named in the parish records.</strong> Two Uruguayan
          entries, made for two different children of his, both give them as <em>Luis</em> and
          <em>Justiniana Lenguas</em> &mdash; so <strong>Gilberto and Luis Eugenio are brothers</strong>,
          out of one Montevideo household, and not only in a published tree. Found by searching the
          <em>mother</em>, the one stable name in a family written Lerena, Lorena, <em>Serena</em>,
          Languas and Lenquas. The same search produced three more siblings: Justinia (1843), Alberto
          Otario (1850) and Mar&iacute;a Ema (1857).</li>
        <li><strong>Luis Eugenio&rsquo;s death act was read</strong> &mdash; 24 December 1901, act 216
          &mdash; and it names his father <em>Luis Lerena</em>, dead by then, and his mother
          <em>Justiniana Lenguas</em>, <strong>alive at seventy-nine</strong>. It gives his trade as
          <em><strong>hacendado</strong></em>: a landowner.</li>
        <li><strong>The marriage, the seven children and Julia&rsquo;s own baptism</strong> are all now
          on primary or near-primary footing &mdash; including act 1720 of 1886, which names
          <em>four grandparents in a single line</em>.</li>
      </ul>
      <div class="note" style="margin-top:14px"><strong>So the tiering in the paragraph above is
        superseded.</strong> The generations above the 1866 act no longer rest on Geneanet and a blog
        &mdash; they rest on Montevideo parish and civil registers.</div>
      <div class="panel" style="border-left:3px solid var(--accent);margin:16px 0">
        <p style="margin:0"><strong>And a sentence that stood here for a day was wrong.</strong> It said
          Gilberto&rsquo;s parentage was <em>&ldquo;still index-level: two entries agree and neither image
          has been opened&rdquo;</em>. One of them was opened about an hour after that was written.
          <strong>Act n&ordm; 1447, San Francisco de As&iacute;s, 31 October 1886</strong>, names the
          paternal grandparents of Gilberto&rsquo;s daughter as <em>&ldquo;Don Luis y Do&ntilde;a
          Justiniana Lenguas&rdquo;</em> &mdash; the couple named as Luis Eugenio&rsquo;s parents in two
          other acts. <strong>The brotherhood is primary.</strong> And the godmother at that font was
          <em>Do&ntilde;a Julia Juanic&oacute; de Lerena</em>, Luis Eugenio&rsquo;s own wife.
          <a href={u("/horses/")}>The act &rarr;</a></p>
      </div>
      <p class="cap" style="margin-top:14px"><strong>And the hypothesis itself is untouched.</strong>
        None of this finds Juan Carlos &mdash; he is in none of the five siblings and in no indexed
        record in Argentina or Uruguay &mdash; and none of it connects Pablo Armando to that household.
        What moved is the standing of the Uruguayan claim, not the descent.
        <a href={u("/horses/")}>The horses &rarr;</a> &middot;
        <a href={u("/searched/")}>What was read &rarr;</a></p>
    </div>
  </div>
  <div class="panel" style="margin:22px 0">
    <p class="lab" style="margin:0 0 8px">And a base rate, from evidence already on the shelf</p>
    <div class="prose">
      <p>Pablo Armando&rsquo;s enrolment card lives in army drawer <strong>&ldquo;Cajón 985
        (Lera)&rdquo;</strong>, which is filed <strong>by sound</strong> &mdash; so its Lerena section is
        a near-complete roll of Argentine Lerena men of enrolment age, <strong>each card naming his
        father and mother</strong>. It was read on 10 September to confirm that no card but his names a
        Juan Carlos and a María. It still doesn&rsquo;t. But the <em>fathers</em> were never followed up.
        Three of them trace:</p>
    </div>
    <div class="scroll" style="margin-top:14px">
      <table class="rec">
        <tr><th>The father on the cards</th><th>His wife</th><th>Where <em>he</em> was born</th></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Gilberto Lerena</strong></td><td>Julia Salvanach</td>
          <td><strong>Montevideo, 24 May 1855</strong> &mdash; Gilberto Justiniano Lerena Lenguas</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Alejandro M. Lerena</strong></td><td>Julia Bassani</td>
          <td><strong>Uruguay, 1839</strong> &mdash; and a son born at Montevideo, 1886</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Nemesio Ramón Lerena</strong></td><td>Laura Escobite</td>
          <td>married at <strong>Salto, Uruguay</strong>, 1903 &mdash; then Concordia, Entre Ríos, across
            the river</td></tr>
      </table>
    </div>
    <div class="prose" style="margin-top:14px">
      <p>Three branches, settling in Entre Ríos, Misiones and Buenos Aires, and <strong>one common
        origin</strong> across the river. Four more fathers could not be traced either way.</p>
      <p class="cap"><strong>This table said four until the evening of 13 September</strong>, and carried
        a row for <em>Arturo Lerena and Amalia Díaz</em>, born Montevideo 1858, son of Avelino Lerena and
        Ambrosia Traibel. That was two men run together: the Uruguayan record it rested on gives the
        wife as <em>Ema Regules</em>, who married <strong>Juan Arturo</strong> Lerena Traibel
        (1861&ndash;1936), while the Arturo in the drawer married Amalia Díaz and had children in La
        Pampa, Mendoza and Entre Ríos. The row is withdrawn.
        <a href={u("/corrections/")}>The correction &rarr;</a></p>
      <p><strong>It is not a link</strong>; none of these men is Juan Carlos Lerena. It is a
        <strong>base rate</strong>: in this generation an Argentine Lerena is a Uruguayan family&rsquo;s
        son far more often than not &mdash; in every traceable case in the one drawer where they are
        gathered together. Juan Carlos Lerena, who left no trace whatever in the province where his son
        was born, fits that shape.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
  <div class="panel" style="border-left:3px solid var(--accent);margin:22px 0">
    <p class="lab" style="margin:0 0 8px">13 September, later &mdash; the Montevideo household opened
      from the marriage to the last burial</p>
    <div class="prose">
      <p>Full text was given the phrase <strong>&ldquo;Lerena Lenguas&rdquo;</strong>. Seven hits in the
        whole corpus, all Montevideo, and between them they open the entire household.</p>
      <p><strong>San Francisco de Asís, Ciudad Vieja, act Nº 258</strong> &mdash; read from the image:</p>
      <p style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.05rem;color:var(--ink-2)">
        <em>En diez y nueve de Diciembre de mil ochocientos sesenta y cuatro &hellip; contrajo
        <strong>Don Luis Lerena y Lenguas</strong>, natural de esta República, soltero, é
        <strong>hijo legítimo de Don Luis y de Doña Justiniana Lenguas</strong>, con
        <strong>Doña Julia Juanicó</strong> &hellip; é hija legítima del <strong>Sr. Don Cándido</strong>
        y de <strong>Doña Juliana González</strong>.</em></p>
      <p><strong>That is the join the whole Montevideo argument rested on, and this morning it rested on
        a tree.</strong> Geneanet and the FamilySearch Tree both asserted this parentage and neither
        cited anything. The act states it. <strong>The generation above Luis Lerena Lenguas is no longer
        secondary.</strong></p>
      <p>It also names Julia Juanicó&rsquo;s parents for the first time &mdash; <strong>Dr Don Cándido
        Juanicó</strong>, who stood as a witness at his own daughter&rsquo;s wedding, and Doña Juliana
        González. And the date fits without strain: married 19 December 1864, first child born at
        Rosario 8 October 1865.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="scroll" style="margin-top:14px">
      <table class="rec">
        <tr><th>The four children</th><th></th></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>María Julia Margarita Josefa Emirena Justiniana</strong></td>
          <td>b. <strong>Rosario</strong>, 8 Oct 1865 · m. Jaime Herrera, Montevideo, 1886</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Luis</strong></td><td>b. c.1872 · d. Montevideo, 12 Dec 1897, aged 25, unmarried</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Enrique</strong></td><td>b. Montevideo c.1873 · d. Montevideo, 16 May 1914, aged 41,
          unmarried, an <em>empleado</em></td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Ema Sixta</strong></td><td>b. 1876 · m. José Pedro Marsera, La Unión, Montevideo, 1913</td></tr>
      </table>
    </div>
    <div class="prose" style="margin-top:14px">
      <p><strong>Ema Sixta is the &ldquo;Emma Liseta, 1876, father Luis, mother Julia&rdquo;</strong>
        copied from a findmypast index on 9 September and left unremarked &mdash; four days before anyone
        knew whose daughter she was.</p>
      <p><strong>And Juan Carlos Lerena is not among them.</strong> The one Lerena family documented in
        the River Plate in this generation has been opened from the marriage to the last burial, and he
        is not in it. That costs this hypothesis nothing &mdash; it never claimed Pablo Armando descended
        from <em>this</em> household, only that the surname&rsquo;s River Plate home is Montevideo. What
        has changed is that the household is now fully mapped, so nobody need wonder about it again.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
  <div class="note" style="margin-top:20px">
    <strong>And the negative, which is worth as much.</strong> The indexed Uruguayan record was searched
    the same evening. There is <strong>no Juan Carlos Lerena</strong> born in Uruguay between 1830 and
    1870 in <em>Uruguay, Catholic Church Records 1726&ndash;2000</em>, in <em>Uruguay, Civil Registration
    1879&ndash;2020</em>, or in the <em>Uruguay Baptisms 1750&ndash;1900</em> index; and <strong>no
    Lerena groom marrying a María</strong> in Uruguay between 1865 and 1885. Every <em>Juan Carlos
    Lerena</em> the index returns is a twentieth-century civil-registration card, decades too late.
    <br /><br />
    The record does not hand him over. What that narrows is <strong>which un-indexed pages are worth
    turning</strong>: the Montevideo parish registers, image by image, the way Rosario was read.
  </div>
  <p><span class="chip">Strong lead, no link proved &mdash; 13 September 2026</span>
    <a href={u("/searched/")}>What was read &rarr;</a></p>
</section>
<section class="blk">
<h2 id="h-2-how-an-argentine-came-to-be-on-a-ship-out-of-england">2. How an Argentine came to be on a ship out of England</h2>
  <p><strong>That the River Plate reached the Cape <em>through Europe</em>, so he need only have changed ships at Southampton.</strong></p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>For:</strong> in the 1900s there was no dense direct Buenos Aires&ndash;Cape Town passenger
      service; sailing to Europe and transshipping was ordinary. Southampton was the hub of the mail run to
      the Cape. On this reading "he came from Argentina" and "they met on a ship from England" are not in
      conflict at all.</li>
    <li><strong>Also for:</strong> the South African War shipped roughly <strong>360,000 horses</strong>
      into the country, <strong>Argentina among the sources</strong>. He was 17 when it began and 20 when it
      ended, and he was a horseman. He was moving with a trade.</li>
    <li><strong>And for her:</strong> orphaned at 15 and 18, in the very years the Colonial Office and the
      emigration societies were running an organised scheme to send <strong>single British women to South
      Africa</strong>.</li>
    <li><strong>To settle it:</strong> <strong>UK outward passenger lists (BT 27)</strong>, indexed from
      1890. Note that she was <strong>Taylor until September 1911</strong> and they may have travelled
      separately.</li>
  </ul>
  <p><span class="chip lore">The meeting itself is family lore</span> <a href={u("/taylor/")}>The Taylors &rarr;</a></p>
</section>
<section class="blk">
<h2 id="h-3-the-consulship-and-it-may-be-the-wrong-country">3. The consulship &mdash; and it may be the wrong country</h2>
  <p><strong>That the consular post, if it existed, was <em>Uruguayan</em> and not Argentine.</strong></p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>For:</strong> the family's own record carries an occupation entry
      <strong>"Uruguay consulate, Cape Town"</strong>, and a note that he was <em>"the ambassador for
      Argentina for a while — although <strong>Yvonne Lerena claims it was Uruguay</strong>."</em></li>
    <li><strong>Why it matters:</strong> this archive has searched the Argentine side and found nothing, and
      on the 1940 Argentine passport he is plainly the <em>bearer</em>, not the issuer. If the post was
      Uruguayan, both facts stop being puzzling at once.</li>
    <li><strong>And it fits the name.</strong> <a href={u("/name/")}>Uruguay has the densest Lerena
      population on earth</a> — a small country where the name is common enough to make a resident Lerena a
      natural choice for an honorary post.</li>
    <li><strong>To settle it:</strong> the Uruguayan consulate at Cape Town — its officers, and the
      <em>exequatur</em> recognising them in the Union Government Gazette. A far narrower search than the
      Argentine one, and untried.</li>
  </ul>
  <p><span class="chip">Newly opened, and the better lead</span></p>
</section>
<section class="blk">
<h2 id="h-3b-or-the-tradition-grew-out-of-the-passport-itself">3b. Or the tradition grew out of the passport itself</h2>
  <p><strong>That "he was the Consul General" is a family memory of the family's Argentine consular passport.</strong></p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>For:</strong> the 1940 passport is a striking object, embossed, sealed and issued by the
      <em>Consulado General Argentino</em> at Cape Town — exactly the sort of heirloom a family remembers as
      a connection to the consulate. On the document itself he is plainly the <strong>bearer</strong>; the
      consular signature belongs to <strong>A. B. Bayne</strong>.</li>
    <li><strong>The alternative still open:</strong> an <strong>honorary</strong> consulship at some other
      date. Resident foreign nationals of standing were commonly appointed, and it would leave a trace in
      the host state's gazette but not in Argentine career foreign-service lists.</li>
    <li><strong>To settle it:</strong> the <em>exequatur</em> in the Union of South Africa Government
      Gazette; the Argentine <em>Boletín Oficial</em>; and the Cape Town consulate's staff lists across
      1900&ndash;1950 (look for A. B. Bayne to date the incumbency).</li>
  </ul>
  <div class="note" style="margin-top:20px">
    <strong>10 September 2026 &mdash; a date that gives the story a mechanism.</strong>
    Argentina and South Africa <strong>established diplomatic relations only on 10 September 1947</strong>.
    South Africa opened a consulate at Buenos Aires in 1938; Argentina opened its legation at Pretoria in
    1950. Before 1938 the published histories record nothing.
    <br /><br />
    Yet this family holds two Argentine documents proving a consular office at Cape Town in
    <strong>1908</strong> (the enrolment card&rsquo;s <em>&ldquo;Oficina enroladora de Capetown&rdquo;</em>)
    and in <strong>1940</strong> (the passport&rsquo;s <em>Oficina Consular Argentina</em>, with seal and
    a 16.50-peso consular stamp). <strong>Thirty-nine years before there were diplomatic relations, and
    thirty before the first consulate the histories mention.</strong>
    <br /><br />
    That is not a contradiction &mdash; consular relations are not diplomatic relations, and the usual
    instrument is the <strong>honorary consul</strong>: a resident merchant, unpaid, commissioned to stamp
    passports and register nationals. Such posts predate legations by decades and leave almost no trace.
    <em>A. B. Bayne</em> is not a Spanish name.
    <br /><br />
    <strong>So the tradition is not proved, but it is now explicable</strong> &mdash; which is a different
    and more honest thing. There <em>was</em> an Argentine consular office at the Cape, it ran for at least
    thirty-two years, and it was almost certainly honorary and locally staffed. In a city with a handful
    of Argentine nationals, the man who held the country&rsquo;s passport and dealt with its consulate is
    exactly the man a family remembers as &ldquo;something with the consulate&rdquo;.
  </div>
  <p style="margin-top:18px"><span class="chip inf">The claim as told is disputed</span>
    <a href={u("/passport/")}>The passport &rarr;</a></p>
</section>
<section class="blk">
<h2 id="h-4-the-ostrich-farm">4. The ostrich farm</h2>
  <p><strong>That there was no ostrich farm, and the tradition has attached itself to the wrong man or the wrong country.</strong></p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>For:</strong> a sweep of South African deeds registers finds him holding
      <strong>no land at all</strong>. Every document places him in Cape Town racing — stables at Rugby, a
      training yard, "race horse owner" at death, "horse-dealer" at marriage.</li>
    <li><strong>Against dismissing it:</strong> deeds coverage is partial; a farm could have been
      <strong>leased</strong>, leaving no transfer; and <strong>Oudtshoorn's own records have not been
      searched</strong>. Ostriches were also farmed in Argentina's near relative, the rhea — a tradition
      could have crossed with him.</li>
    <li><strong>To settle it:</strong> Oudtshoorn and Little Karoo farm and lease records; the C.P. Nel
      Museum; the Cape deeds registry under both <em>Lerena</em> and <em>Lerina</em>.</li>
  </ul>
  <p><span class="chip inf">Doubtful, not disproved</span> <a href={u("/south-africa/")}>South Africa &rarr;</a></p>
</section>
<section class="blk">
<h2 id="h-5-where-pablo-armando-s-land-was">5. Where Pablo Armando's land was</h2>
  <p><strong>That he held land at Blouberg, which the deeds sweep failed to find.</strong></p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>For:</strong> the family record states plainly that <em>"he owned a lot of land up around
      the <strong>Blouberg</strong> area"</em> — the coast north of Cape Town, beyond Milnerton and Rugby,
      where his stables were.</li>
    <li><strong>Against:</strong> a search of the South African deeds registers returned
      <strong>no land at all</strong> in his name.</li>
    <li><strong>Most likely explanation:</strong> the search, not the claim, is at fault. Deeds indexing is
      partial and his surname is written <em>Lerena</em>, <em>Lerina</em> and <em>Larena</em> across
      different records.</li>
    <li><strong>To settle it:</strong> the Cape deeds registry for the Blouberg and Milnerton areas, under
      every spelling.</li>
  </ul>
  <p><span class="chip">Open — and it bears on the ostrich farm above</span></p>
</section>
<section class="blk">
<h2 id="h-6-that-he-left-argentina-as-a-political-refugee">6. That he left Argentina as a political refugee</h2>
  <div class="note" style="margin:16px 0"><strong>10 September 2026 — answered from Amsterdam, and it
    goes against the story.</strong> The <strong>International Institute of Social History</strong>, the
    principal repository for the international left, searched its OCR&rsquo;d corpus of Latin American
    anarchist and labour periodicals, 1880&ndash;1940: <em>&ldquo;nothing popped up.&rdquo;</em> Re-run
    here the same day and the null holds &mdash; the 171 apparent &ldquo;Lerena&rdquo; hits are the
    <em>Conde de Lerena</em> of the 1787 Spanish census and <em>Osimani y Llerena</em>, a street in
    Montevideo where an anarchist weekly had its office; the 16 &ldquo;Lerina&rdquo; hits are OCR breakage
    of <em>ballerina</em>.
    <br /><br />
    <strong>But that is the weakest of the three objections, and it should not be dressed up.</strong> A
    horse-dealer in his twenties would not be in the anarchist press unless he were notable, and most
    people never appear in any periodical. <strong>The real objection is the enrolment card:</strong> in
    1908 he walked into his own government&rsquo;s consular office at Cape Town and wrote down his name,
    his conscript class, his birthplace and both his parents. A man hiding from the Argentine state does
    not do that. And <strong>Ley 4144 deported foreigners, not nationals</strong> &mdash; the mechanism
    the story implies could not have been used on him.
    <br /><br />
    <strong>Not disproved. Now unlikely.</strong> The AGN expulsion files remain the only place that could
    settle it positively.</div>
  <p><strong>That Pablo Armando fled Argentina because he was in political trouble on the left.</strong></p>
  <p class="cap">Family testimony, carried three generations and repeated to David by his mother, Cheryl
    Anne Lerena, in September 2026: that he &ldquo;ran away from Argentina as he was a communist on the
    run.&rdquo; Never proven.</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Why it deserves better than folklore:</strong> it is the <em>only</em> story the family
      carries that explains a <em>departure</em>. Rosario, the horses, the consulship, the ostriches — all
      describe what he was. This alone says why he left, and a reason to leave is precisely what the
      documentary record has refused to supply.</li>
    <li><strong>The correction the story needs:</strong> the Argentine Communist Party did not exist until
      <strong>1918</strong>. He left around 1903–05. The radical movement in Argentina then was
      overwhelmingly <strong>anarchist and anarcho-syndicalist</strong>. Families routinely retrofit
      &ldquo;communist&rdquo; onto earlier left politics once that becomes the available word — so the
      claim to test is &ldquo;in political trouble on the left,&rdquo; not the literal one.</li>
    <li><strong>The mechanism that would make it true:</strong> <strong>Ley 4144, the <em>Ley de
      Residencia</em></strong> of November 1902, which let the government deport any foreign-born person
      deemed a threat <strong>without trial or appeal</strong>. It was used hard against anarchist
      organisers in exactly 1903–05. And <strong>Rosario</strong> — great river port, rail head, big dock
      and railway workforce — was among the most militant anarchist cities in the country.</li>
    <li><strong>The chronology fits tightly.</strong> Born February 1882; his son Roque born at Cape Town
      in November 1905. He left Argentina somewhere in <strong>1903–05</strong>, aged 21 to 23.</li>
    <li><strong>And here is the part that may matter most.</strong> The Ley de Residencia applied to
      <strong>foreigners</strong>. An Argentine national could be prosecuted but not deported under it. So
      if he was <em>expelled</em>, <strong>he was not Argentine-born</strong> — and our failure to find him
      in the Rosario baptism register stops being a puzzle and becomes a consequence. That would sit with
      the Uruguayan density of the surname, the Uruguayan-born Lerenas in the 1895 census, and the family
      note that the consulship may have been Uruguayan.</li>
    <li><strong>Against:</strong> nothing documentary supports it yet, and he was issued an Argentine
      passport in 1940 — so he was neither stateless nor permanently barred. If he merely fled prosecution
      rather than being deported, he could be Argentine-born after all.</li>
    <li><strong>To settle it:</strong> Ministerio del Interior expulsion files at the Archivo General de la
      Nación; Santa Fe provincial and Rosario police records 1902–05; the anarchist press, chiefly
      <em>La Protesta</em>, much of it held at the International Institute of Social History in Amsterdam.
      None has been asked.</li>
  </ul>
  <p><span class="chip lore">Family lore — untested, falsifiable, and pointing at named archives</span></p>
</section>
<section class="blk">
<h2 id="h-7-the-llorens-baptism">7. The Llorens baptism</h2>
  <p><strong>That Pablo Armando descends from the Roberto Llorens and Francisca Fernández named in a San Miguel baptism of 1872.</strong></p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Why it was ever entertained:</strong> the child baptised there was
      <strong>Roberto Juan Roque</strong>, and both <em>Roberto</em> and <em>Roque</em> recur in this
      family — a <strong>Roberto Juan Roque Lerena</strong> was born in 1944.</li>
    <li><strong>Against:</strong> the surname is <strong>Llorens</strong>, a Catalan name meaning Lawrence,
      not a variant of Lerena; and the streets and parish point to <strong>Buenos Aires</strong>, not
      Rosario. <strong>David Defranceski states plainly that these records are unproven</strong>, and this
      archive treats them so.</li>
    <li><strong>To settle it:</strong> Pablo Armando's own baptism at Rosario, spring 1882, which would name
      his parents outright and end the question.</li>
    <li><strong>What happened when it was tested:</strong> the Rosario index has now been read across
      <strong>all twenty-six letters for 1882</strong>, and there is <strong>no Pablo Armando under any
      surname</strong> — the F section, where a Fernández registration would sit, is clear. That does not
      make the Llorens baptism false; it removes the one route that could have connected it to him.</li>
  </ul>
  <p><span class="chip inf">Unproven; not cited elsewhere on this site</span></p>
</section>
<section class="blk">
<h2 id="h-8-a-carmen-in-the-argentine-line">8. A Carmen in the Argentine line</h2>
  <p><strong>That the name <em>Carmen</em>, surviving into 1958, descends from Pablo Armando's own mother or grandmother.</strong></p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Against:</strong> <em>Carmen</em> is among the commonest female names in the
      Spanish-speaking world — a devotional name from <em>Nuestra Señora del Carmen</em>. This family was
      Catholic; a devotional choice needs no ancestor to explain it.</li>
    <li><strong>For:</strong> look at what the family actually used. The <em>male</em> Spanish names are
      abundant and persistent across three South African generations — Pablo Armando, Roque Luis Armando,
      Ricardo Juan Carlos, Nuno Fernando, Roberto Juan Roque, Anton Armando, Juan Carlos, Gilberto. The
      <em>female</em> ones are scarce, and <strong>Carmen</strong> is the clearest survival among them.</li>
    <li><strong>And the grandmothers do not account for it.</strong> Patricia Carmen Lerena's grandmothers
      were <a href={u("/taylor/")}>Mary Septima Taylor</a>, an Englishwoman of Devon, and the mother of
      Catherine Mary Sophia Booyzen, an Afrikaner. Neither was a Carmen. Spanish custom names children for
      grandparents; if this name descends rather than being chosen fresh, it descends from
      <strong>further back on the Lerena side</strong>.</li>
    <li><strong>Use:</strong> weak as proof, useful as a filter. In Argentine and Uruguayan Lerena records,
      <strong>give extra weight to any household containing a Carmen</strong>.</li>
  </ul>
  <p><span class="chip">Inference from naming — modest, and worth carrying</span></p>
</section>
<section class="blk">
<h2 id="h-9-the-remaining-smaller-ones">9. The remaining smaller ones</h2>
  <div class="scroll">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th style="width:20rem">Hypothesis</th><th>Standing</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td><strong>The 1879 twin was a girl</strong></td>
          <td>Required by the arithmetic of "Septima": Mary Septima is only the seventh daughter if the twin
            was female. <span class="chip">Sound but formally unproved</span></td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>The family was regularised in the church in 1911</strong></td>
          <td>Son baptised 12 June 1911; parents married 3 September 1911, both bachelor and spinster; the
            marriage examined by a "Bert…" who is very likely Bertram W. Glynn, the priest who performed the
            baptism. <span class="chip">Strong</span></td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Gavin Lerena's descent</strong></td>
          <td>Pablo Armando &rarr; Ricardo &rarr; Carlos "Tex" &rarr; Gavin, per the family chart, and the
            racing press independently describes the same succession of trainers.
            <span class="chip">Family chart, not independently verified</span></td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>He imported horses for the government</strong></td>
          <td>The family record states he <em>"used to import horses into South Africa from Argentina for
            the ZA government"</em>, and separately for his own stables. This is precisely the testable form
            of the horse claim that this archive set out on day one.
            <span class="chip lore">Family testimony; find the contracts</span></td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>He left Argentina for a political reason</strong></td>
          <td>The family record says he <em>"ran from Argentina for a communism related reason"</em> and
            flags it as unsubstantiated. He was Argentine <em>by birth</em> and served in the National
            Guard, so this would place his departure after 1915.
            <span class="chip lore">Unsubstantiated</span></td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>The family concealed the illegitimacy</strong></td>
          <td>The family record notes that on a <strong>copy document of 1916</strong> the marriage
            <em>"dates have been manipulated to show an earlier year… but it was 1911"</em> — which would
            place the wedding before Roque's 1905 birth.
            <span class="chip">Strong, and the 1916 copy is worth finding</span></td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Two men called "Bob"</strong></td>
          <td>The family record gives the nickname <em>Bob</em> to <strong>both</strong> Pablo Armando and
            his son Ricardo. The Sporting Post's "Bob Lerena, grandfather of Spike" must still be Pablo
            Armando, since Ricardo was Spike's <em>father</em> — but the ambiguity is a live hazard in
            racing records. <span class="chip inf">A trap to watch for</span></td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
</section>
<section class="blk">
<h2 id="h-retired">Retired</h2>
  <p>
    <span class="chip doc">Proved</span> <strong>That Mary Septima Taylor was born in England.</strong>
    This archive doubted it for part of one day, on the strength of a badly filtered search that returned no
    English record. The family's own sourced tree carries the answer: <strong>born at Bideford, Devon,
    17 September 1884</strong>, cited to the England and Wales birth registration index and the GRO at
    Southport, and christened at St Margaret's, Northam. The doubt was mine and it was wrong.
    <a href={u("/taylor/")}>The Taylors &rarr;</a>
  </p>
  <p>
    <span class="chip doc">Proved</span> <strong>That "Rosalina Wilhelmina Chappell" was a previous married
    name.</strong> This archive proposed it as one of two readings. The family record settles it: Roque
    married <strong>Rose Forbes, who was Doreen Chappell's mother</strong> — so Chappell was Rose's name by
    her first marriage, and Roque married his own brother's mother-in-law.
    <a href={u("/horses/")}>The horses &rarr;</a>
  </p>
  <p>
    <span class="chip inf">Disproved</span> <strong>That Pablo Armando Lerena and Robert Paul Lerena were
    brothers.</strong> This archive proposed it for one day, on the ground that the names and ages would not
    reconcile. The Corpus Christi baptism register named <strong>Pablo Armando</strong> as the father of the
    son whose death notice names <strong>Roberto</strong> — one man, two forms of his name. The inference is
    withdrawn. <a href={u("/passport/")}>The passport &rarr;</a>
  </p>
  <p>
    <span class="chip inf">Disproved</span> <strong>That the 61 South African Lerenas are one kindred.</strong>
    At least two unconnected populations bear the name there. <a href={u("/south-africa/")}>South Africa &rarr;</a>
  </p>
</section>
